From: the SK-D840N 7.3 line
Subject: [PATCH] net: zx279133: give the switch engine the vendor's table set

The switch engine's microcode knows a fixed set of tables: for each one an
id, the width of an entry and the number of entries.  The board loads
np_133.ko, and that module's g_core_sdt_info - 53 entries, recovered in
docs/29 and docs/31 - is that set.  The generic np.ko's set, which the 7.3
tree's np.c declared, belongs to a module that is not on this board at all.

Two things follow from declaring the wrong set, and the first one is fatal.
The MF extension table - SDT 0x7d, the mirror of the SPA port attributes,
64 entries of 128 bits - is not in the generic set at all, so the switch
engine was never told about it and the driver had no base for it:
zx279133_9132_port_init() -> zx279133_9132_mf_mirror() ->
zx279133_eram_window_patch() returned -EINVAL, the switch driver's probe
failed with -EINVAL, and the 7.3.4 image came up with no network interface
at all.  The second one is that the tables the two sets share were declared
with another width - 0x1f and 0x22 are statistics tables in the generic set
and ERAM tables in the vendor's - so the hardware would have read them at
another address.

The 6.18 generation already has this right: it creates the vendor's list
and it looks every base up by the table's id, because the base of a table
is not a constant.  The ERAM allocator hands the blocks out in order, out
of a pool that starts at block 12288, so a table's base is the sum of the
depths of the tables before it.  With the vendor's list the pool ends at
block 30273 and the thirteen tables this driver writes land on the
addresses the vendor's own module gives them: 0x18 at 19624, 0x19 at
19752, 0x1b at 19820, 0x22 at 19592, 0x27 at 24071 and 0x7d at 24737.

So this patch takes the vendor's list, the three tables of the driver's own
(0x00, 0x04 and 0x06, which the vendor's list does not have) after it, and
the four hard-coded base blocks with it.  It also moves the MF extension
entry's port attribute word from word 3 to word 0: the vendor's
port_attr_def_*_set() helpers patch the byte at offset 3 of the entry and
write the entry back, and the board's own ERAM dump (docs/31) shows the
attribute word 0x96040001 in word 0 with the other three words empty.

The one entry that belonged to a table the vendor does not have - the old
sdt 0x10 - is gone, and the factory's port type entries now follow the
port type table's base instead of a block number.

